			"id":"539e4662748ce219",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"# The Cognitive Turn in Recommender Systems: From Generative Retrieval to System-2 Reasoning and Conformal Safety\n\n## 1. Introduction: The Post-Discriminative Era\n\nThe trajectory of Recommender Systems (RecSys) research has historically been characterized by the pursuit of increasingly sophisticated matching functions. For nearly two decades, the field was dominated by a discriminative paradigm: given a user $u$ and an item $i$, the system’s primary objective was to estimate a scalar score $\\hat{y}_{ui}$ representing the probability of interaction. This paradigm evolved from the linearity of Matrix Factorization (MF) in the late 2000s to the non-linear feature interactions of Neural Collaborative Filtering (NCF) and ultimately to the massive Deep Learning Recommendation Models (DLRM) that currently underpin the majority of industrial applications.\n\nHowever, as we analyze the research landscape of late 2024 through early 2026—synthesizing the proceedings of ACM RecSys 2025, SIGIR 2025, and KDD 2025, along with the calls for papers for their 2026 iterations—it is evident that the field is undergoing a foundational phase transition. We are exiting the discriminative era and entering what can be termed the \"Generative and Cognitive Era.\" This shift is driven not merely by the incremental application of Large Language Models (LLMs) as feature encoders, but by a rethinking of the recommendation task itself: from \"ranking existing items\" to \"generating user-centric plans.\"\n\n### 1.1 The Convergence of Retrieval and Ranking\n\nThe traditional industrial standard, often referred to as the Multi-stage Cascading Architecture (MCA), separates candidate generation (retrieval) from fine-grained ranking. This separation was necessitated by computational constraints: heavy ranking models could not process millions of items. However, MCA introduced a \"semantic gap\" where the retrieval objective (often recall-oriented) misaligned with the ranking objective (often conversion-oriented).\n\nRecent developments in 2025 demonstrate a collapsing of this cascade. **Generative Recommendation** unifies these stages into a single sequence-to-sequence workflow. By representing items as \"Semantic IDs\" or tokens within a transformer's vocabulary, models can directly generate the target item's identifier from the user's interaction history. This unification allows for end-to-end optimization, where a single model learns to balance breadth (retrieval) and depth (ranking) within its autoregressive generation process.\n\n### 1.2 The Rise of System-2 Reasoning\n\nPerhaps the most profound development is the integration of **System-2 thinking**—Daniel Kahneman’s concept of slow, deliberate, and logical cognition—into recommendation agents. Traditional collaborative filtering operates as System-1: fast, intuitive, and associative (\"People who bought X also bought Y\"). While efficient, System-1 fails in complex, low-data scenarios where reasoning is required.\n\nNew frameworks emerging in 2025, such as **R4ec** and **ThinkRec** , equip recommender systems with the ability to \"think\" before they act. These systems do not merely predict the next click; they formulate a reasoning chain, reflect on its validity, and refine their output. This cognitive capability allows for handling complex user intents (e.g., \"I need a camping setup for rainy weather\") that baffle purely correlational models.\n\n### 1.3 The Imperative of Trustworthiness\n\nAs systems become more agentic and opaque, the risk of \"unwanted recommendations\"—content that maximizes engagement but violates user well-being—has escalated. The research community has responded with rigorous new methodologies for safety. **Conformal Risk Control** has emerged as a dominant theoretical framework in 2025, offering statistical guarantees on the safety of recommendation sets rather than relying on heuristic filters. Simultaneously, the evaluation of explainability has matured, with research exposing the flaws in traditional Top-1 evaluation metrics for counterfactual explanations.\n\nThis report provides an exhaustive analysis of these trends, dissecting the architectures, methodologies, and industrial realities of the current state of the art. Based on this synthesis, we identify a critical, high-impact problem space for future research: **\"Latency-Efficient System-2 Reasoning for Dynamic Item Catalogs.\"**\n\n---\n\n## 2. Generative Recommendation: Unifying the Stack\n\nThe paradigm of Generative Recommendation (GenRec) fundamentally alters the mathematical formulation of the recommendation problem. Instead of learning a scoring function $f(u, i) \\rightarrow \\mathbb{R}$, GenRec learns a conditional probability distribution $P(i | H_u)$ over the item space, where $H_u$ is the user's history. This is achieved by tokenizing items and treating recommendation as a next-token prediction task.\n\n### 2.1 Theoretical Foundations and Architectures\n\nThe transition to generative models is motivated by the limitations of dense retrieval. In dense retrieval (e.g., Two-Tower models), items are mapped to fixed vectors. Retrieval is performed via Maximum Inner Product Search (MIPS). This approach suffers from the \"curse of dimensionality\" and often fails to capture fine-grained semantic nuances.\n\nGenerative recommendation replaces MIPS with **autoregressive generation**. The model generates the item identifier token-by-token. This allows the model to leverage the full power of the Transformer's attention mechanism across the entire retrieval process, effectively performing a \"beam search\" over the item space that serves as a highly sophisticated ranking mechanism.\n\n#### 2.1.1 Encoder-Decoder vs. Decoder-Only\n\nThe 2025 surveys categorize industrial GenRec systems into two primary architectural families:\n\n|**Architecture**|**Description**|**Advantages**|**Industrial Examples**|\n|---|---|---|---|\n|**Encoder-Decoder**|Similar to T5 or BART. The encoder processes the user history and context; the decoder generates the item identifier.|Separates understanding from generation; allows for bi-directional attention on history.|**TIGER** (Google), **LIGER** (Meta), **P5**|\n|**Decoder-Only**|Similar to GPT. User history and item identifiers are concatenated into a single sequence.|Simpler architecture; aligns with standard LLM pre-training objectives; naturally handles sequential recommendation.|**GPT4Rec**, **S3-Rec**, **MTGR**|\n\nThe **MTGR** (Multi-Task Generative Recommendation) framework represents the cutting edge of Decoder-Only architectures. It demonstrates that trillion-parameter sequential transducers can replace entire multi-stage pipelines. By training on a diverse mixture of tasks—sequential recommendation, explanation generation, and item detail completion—MTGR achieves a level of generalization previously unattainable by specialized models.\n\n### 2.2 The Item Tokenization Challenge\n\nThe critical enabler of GenRec is the ability to represent discrete items as sequences of tokens. In Natural Language Processing (NLP), the vocabulary (words/sub-words) is relatively static. In RecSys, the \"vocabulary\" is the item catalog, which is massive, dynamic, and sparse.\n\n#### 2.2.1 Semantic IDs (SIDs)\n\nSimply using integer IDs (e.g., Item #49201) is inefficient because there is no semantic correlation between ID #49201 and ID #49202. To solve this, researchers introduced **Semantic IDs**.\n\n- **Hierarchical Quantization:** Methods like **TIGER** utilize Residual Quantized Variational Autoencoders (RQ-VAE). The item's textual or visual features are encoded into a dense vector, which is then quantized into a tuple of discrete codes (e.g., `(c1, c2, c3)`).\n    \n- **Semantic Structure:** This hierarchical structure ensures that similar items share common prefixes. A pair of Nike running shoes might have the code `(Sports, Shoes, Running, Nike)`, while Adidas running shoes might be `(Sports, Shoes, Running, Adidas)`.\n    \n- **Inductive Bias:** This injects a strong inductive bias into the model: to recommend a \"running shoe,\" the model first generates the token `Sports`, then `Shoes`. This facilitates valid generation even for long-tail items that have been seen rarely during training, as the model learns the category-level transitions robustly.\n    \n\n#### 2.2.2 Token Instability and Dynamic Vocabularies\n\nDespite the elegance of Semantic IDs, 2025 literature identifies **Token Instability** as a severe open problem.\n\n- **The Phenomenon:** As the item catalog evolves—new products are added, old ones removed, and trends shift—the optimal hierarchical clustering changes. A Semantic ID assigned in January might be semantically suboptimal in March.\n    \n- **The Consequence:** To maintain performance, the entire catalog must be re-indexed and the GenRec model re-trained or fine-tuned to adapt to the new codebook. This high-maintenance cycle is a major barrier to real-time industrial deployment.\n    \n- **Emerging Solutions:** Research is currently pivoting toward **Dynamic Vocabulary Adaptation** and **Streaming Quantization**, where new items can be inserted into the semantic codebook without a full rebuild, though this remains an active area of investigation.\n    \n\n### 2.3 Unification of Modalities\n\nThe GenRec paradigm is also driving the adoption of multimodal foundation models. Traditional DLRMs rely on pre-computed feature vectors. In contrast, modern GenRec systems (e.g., **LIGER**, **QARM**) ingest raw text, images, and video frames directly.\n\n- **QARM (Kuaishou):** This system aligns visual, textual, and behavioral features through **quantitative token alignment**. It maps different modalities into a unified token space, allowing the model to reason across modalities (e.g., \"The user watched a video about _cooking_ (visual); recommend a book about _spices_ (text)\").\n    \n- **Implications for Cold Start:** This multimodal capability is the primary driver for GenRec's superiority in cold-start scenarios. Even if an item has zero interactions, its semantic tokens (derived from its content) allow the model to place it correctly within the manifold of user preferences.\n    \n\n---\n\n## 3. The Cognitive Revolution: Reasoning, Reflection, and Agents\n\nWhile Generative Recommendation improves _how_ we predict the next item, the \"Cognitive Revolution\" addresses _why_ we predict it. This marks the transition from correlation-based pattern matching to causal, deliberative planning.\n\n### 3.1 The System-1 vs. System-2 Dichotomy\n\nThe distinction between System-1 (fast, intuitive) and System-2 (slow, deliberative) thinking has been successfully translated into RecSys architectures in 2025.\n\n- **System-1 RecSys:** Maps inputs (User History) directly to outputs (Item ID) via learned shortcuts. Efficient but brittle. Prone to biases and \"hallucination\" of user intent.\n    \n- **System-2 RecSys:** Interposes a reasoning step. Input $\\rightarrow$ **Reasoning Chain** $\\rightarrow$ Output. This allows for intermediate verification and error correction.\n    \n\n### 3.2 Deep Dive: The R4ec Framework\n\nThe **R4ec** (Reasoning, Reflection, and Refinement) framework represents the state-of-the-art in System-2 recommendation. It was developed to address the sensitivity of LLMs to errors in reasoning paths.\n\n#### 3.2.1 Mechanism\n\nR4ec operates via a **two-role paradigm**:\n\n1. **The Actor ($\\pi_{\\theta}$):** This model is responsible for generating the recommendation plan. It analyzes the user's profile and proposes a set of items along with a rationale.\n    \n2. **The Critic (Reflection Model $\\pi_{\\psi}$):** This model evaluates the Actor's proposal. It checks for:\n    \n    - **Factual Consistency:** Does the item actually exist? Does it have the features claimed?\n        \n    - **User Alignment:** Does this recommendation contradict explicit user constraints (e.g., \"I hate horror movies\")?\n        \n    - **Logical Coherence:** Does the reasoning follow sound logic?\n        \n\nIf the Critic identifies a flaw, it generates feedback, and the Actor refines its proposal. This iterative loop continues until the Critic is satisfied or a timeout is reached.\n\n#### 3.2.2 Impact and Results\n\nIn experiments on the **Amazon-Book** and **MovieLens-1M** datasets, R4ec demonstrated superior performance over standard Chain-of-Thought (CoT) baselines. Crucially, the authors report a deployment on a large-scale online advertising platform where R4ec achieved a **2.2% increase in revenue**. In the context of mature ad platforms where gains of 0.1% are considered significant, a 2.2% lift validates the hypothesis that \"slow thinking\" leads to better economic outcomes by increasing relevance and trust.\n\n### 3.3 ThinkRec: Thinking Activation\n\nParallel to R4ec, the **ThinkRec** framework addresses the data paucity problem in reasoning. Most interaction datasets (like Amazon Reviews) contain only $(u, i, r)$ triplets—user, item, rating. They do not contain the \"reasoning\" behind the purchase.\n\nThinkRec introduces a **Thinking Activation Mechanism**:\n\n- **Data Augmentation:** It uses an LLM to synthesize \"Reasoning Traces\" for historical interactions. For example, \"User U bought Item A and B; therefore, U likely values durability.\"\n    \n- **Training:** The recommender is then fine-tuned on these synthetic traces.\n    \n- **Inference:** During serving, the model is prompted to generate a reasoning trace before predicting the item.\n    \n\nThinkRec shifts the model from a \"System-1 matcher\" to a \"System-2 thinker\" by explicitly training it on the causal logic of consumption.\n\n### 3.4 Agentic Recommender Systems\n\nMoving beyond single-step reasoning, **Agentic RecSys** treats recommendation as a multi-turn, interactive task. The **AgentCF** framework models both the User and the Item as autonomous agents.\n\n- **Simulation:** The User Agent simulates the user's browsing behavior, while the Item Agent \"pitches\" its item.\n    \n- **Collaborative Learning:** The recommendation emerges from the interaction between these agents.\n    \n\n#### 3.4.1 AgentRecBench: Evaluation Standards\n\nTo standardize the evaluation of these complex agents, researchers introduced **AgentRecBench** at NeurIPS 2025. This benchmark evaluates agents across three distinct scenarios:\n\n1. **Classic Recommendation:** Standard accuracy metrics (NDCG, Recall).\n    \n2. **Evolving Interests:** The user's preference shifts suddenly (e.g., getting married, moving cities).\n    \n3. **Cold-Start:** New items with no history.\n    \n\n**Key Finding:** Agentic systems significantly outperform traditional baselines in **Cold-Start** and **Evolving Interest** scenarios, where reasoning is paramount. However, they often lag in **Classic** scenarios due to the \"simplicity bias\" of LLMs and **Catastrophic Forgetting**—agents tend to prioritize recent context heavily, losing track of long-term historical patterns.\n\n---\n\n## 4. Trustworthiness and Safety: Mathematical Guarantees\n\nAs recommender systems gain agency, the potential for harm increases. \"Unwanted recommendations\"—such as misinformation, polarizing content, or addictive but low-quality material—are a major focus of 2025 research. The community is moving away from heuristic filters toward rigorous statistical guarantees.\n\n### 4.1 Conformal Risk Control (CRC)\n\nThe paper **\"You Don't Bring Me Flowers: Mitigating Unwanted Recommendations Through Conformal Risk Control\"** introduces a breakthrough method for safety. Traditional safety classifiers output a probability score (e.g., $P(\\text{Harmful}) = 0.4$). Platforms must arbitrarily decide a threshold (e.g., 0.5).\n\n**Conformal Risk Control** replaces this arbitrary threshold with a calibrated one.\n\n- **Calibration Set:** A held-out set of data $(X_i, Y_i)$ where $Y_i$ indicates if the item was truly \"unwanted\" (e.g., reported by the user).\n    \n- **The Guarantee:** The method computes a threshold $\\lambda$ such that, with high probability (e.g., $1-\\delta$), the expected loss (fraction of unwanted items) in future recommendation sets is bounded by $\\alpha$ (e.g., 5%).\n    \n    $$\\mathbb{P}(R(\\mathcal{T}_{\\lambda}) \\le \\alpha) \\ge 1 - \\delta$$\n    \n    Where $R$ is the risk function and $\\mathcal{T}_{\\lambda}$ is the set of items selected by threshold $\\lambda$.\n    \n\n#### 4.1.1 The Safe Set Strategy\n\nStrict risk control often leads to empty or very short recommendation lists, which degrades user experience. To counter this, the authors propose a **Safe Set** strategy.\n\n- **Re-consumption:** The system identifies items the user has previously consumed and rated positively (high watch time, explicit like).\n    \n- **Backfill:** If the CRC filter removes risky new items, the slots are filled with these \"safe\" re-consumption items.\n    \n- **Result:** This ensures the user always receives a full slate of recommendations that satisfies the safety guarantee, balancing exploration (risky new items) with exploitation (safe old items).\n    \n\n### 4.2 Beyond Top-1: Counterfactual Evaluation\n\nExplainability is a key component of trust. A common technique is **Counterfactual Explanation (CE)**: \"If you hadn't watched X, we wouldn't have recommended Y.\"\n\nThe paper **\"Beyond Top-1: Addressing Inconsistencies in Evaluating Counterfactual Explanations\"** identifies a critical flaw in how CEs are evaluated.\n\n- **The Flaw:** Most research evaluates CEs on the Top-1 item. However, users see a Top-K list. An explanation that explains the Top-1 item might be contradicted by the Top-2 item.\n    \n- **List-Wise Metrics:** The authors introduce **POS@K** and **NEG@K** metrics.\n    \n    - **POS@K:** Measures the fraction of the Top-K list that changes when the \"cause\" item is removed.\n        \n    - **NEG@K:** Measures whether the \"explained\" item remains in the Top-K under irrelevant perturbations.\n        \n- **Finding:** Evaluation metrics must be treated as tunable hyperparameters. A CE method that looks optimal at $K=1$ often degrades significantly at $K=10$, revealing that many \"state-of-the-art\" explainers are less robust than previously thought.\n    \n\n---\n\n## 5. Industrial Realities: Challenges and Constraints\n\nWhile academia pushes the boundaries of reasoning and generation, industrial practitioners face severe constraints defined by physics (latency), economics (cost), and regulation (privacy). The **Survey of Real-World Recommender Systems (2025)** provides a stark look at these gaps.\n\n### 5.1 The Deployment Gap: Latency vs. Reasoning\n\nThe primary barrier to deploying System-2 agents is **inference latency**.\n\n- **The Math:** A typical System-2 chain-of-thought might require generating 200 tokens. If an LLM generates at 50 tokens/second, that is 4 seconds of latency.\n    \n- **The Constraint:** Real-Time Bidding (RTB) and user-facing feeds typically have a latency budget of **<100ms** (often <20ms for the scoring phase).\n    \n- **The Conflict:** There is a 40x gap between the speed of reasoning models and the requirement of production systems.\n    \n\n#### 5.1.1 Distillation and its Limits\n\nThe current industrial stop-gap is **Distillation**. A large Teacher Model (System-2) generates synthetic data (reasoning traces, pseudo-labels), which is used to train a small Student Model (System-1 DLRM).\n\n- **Limit:** This transfers the _knowledge_ but not the _reasoning capability_. The student learns \"User X likes Y,\" but it does not learn _how to think_. Consequently, the student fails in out-of-distribution (OOD) scenarios where the teacher would have succeeded.\n    \n\n### 5.2 Signal Loss and Federated Learning\n\nPrivacy regulations (GDPR, DMA) and platform changes (Apple ATT, cookie deprecation) have caused massive **Signal Loss**. The granular user tracking that powered DLRMs is vanishing.\n\n**Federated Learning (FL)** is gaining traction as a solution.\n\n- **PPLR Framework:** The **Privacy-Preserving LLM-based Recommendation (PPLR)** framework allows for fine-tuning recommendation models on user devices.\n    \n- **Mechanism:** Users keep their raw data. The model weights are sent to the device, updated via local gradients, and sent back.\n    \n- **Challenges:**\n    \n    - **Communication Overhead:** LLMs are huge. Transmitting gradients for a 7B parameter model is feasible over 5G but costly.\n        \n    - **Privacy Attacks:** Even gradients can leak information. FL systems are vulnerable to **Model Poisoning** and **Inference Attacks**, necessitating robust differential privacy mechanisms that further degrade utility.\n        \n\n### 5.3 Coherent vs. Revealed Preference\n\nA subtle but critical conceptual shift is highlighted in the **Coherent Preference (CP)** literature.\n\n- **Revealed Preference (RP):** The assumption that \"User clicked X, therefore User likes X.\" This has been the axiom of RecSys.\n    \n- **Reality:** Users click on clickbait, they click by accident, or they click due to transient states (boredom). RP is noisy.\n    \n- **Coherent Preference:** The assumption that true preferences are logically consistent with the user's history and values.\n    \n- **C-APO Framework:** The **Conflict-Aware Direct Preference Optimization** framework trains LLMs to balance RP and CP. If a click (RP) contradicts the user's established history (CP), the model down-weights that signal. This is crucial for long-term user satisfaction and alignment.\n    \n\n---\n\n## 6. Key Problem Identification: Latency-Efficient System-2 Reasoning\n\nBased on the exhaustive review of the 2025-2026 landscape, a clear \"Blue Ocean\" research opportunity emerges. The field has solved \"Accuracy\" (via DLRM) and \"Reasoning\" (via R4ec/Agents), but it has not solved them _simultaneously_ under industrial constraints.\n\n### 6.1 Problem Statement\n\n**\"Bridging the Gap: Latency-Efficient System-2 Reasoning for Dynamic Item Catalogs.\"**\n\nThe objective is to develop a recommender architecture that retains the deliberative, planning capabilities of System-2 agents but operates within the sub-100ms latency budget of industrial systems, while robustly handling the inevitable evolution of the item catalog (Token Instability).\n\n### 6.2 Detailed Research Questions (RQs)\n\n#### RQ1: Can we decouple Reasoning from Autoregressive Generation?\n\nCurrent System-2 models are slow because they generate reasoning as _text_. Text is a high-dimensional, sparse, and inefficient medium for internal machine communication.\n\n- **Hypothesis:** We can develop **\"Latent Reasoning Planning.\"** Instead of generating the token sequence \"User wants a comedy movie,\" the model generates a continuous **Concept Vector** $z_{plan}$ in a latent space. This vector is then used to query a dense item index.\n    \n- **Methodology:** Train an encoder to map (History, Context) $\\rightarrow$ $z_{plan}$. Train a projector to map $z_{plan}$ $\\rightarrow$ Item Embeddings. This avoids the $O(N)$ cost of autoregressive generation, reducing it to $O(1)$ vector generation + $O(\\log M)$ retrieval.\n    \n\n#### RQ2: How do we solve Token Instability in Reasoning Models?\n\nSystem-2 models like ThinkRec rely on static metadata. When a new item appears, the model doesn't know how to reason about it until it is retrained.\n\n- **Hypothesis:** **\"Streaming Inductive Quantization.\"** Develop a lightweight module that ingests a new item's raw features (text/image) and maps it to the existing \"Reasoning Space\" (Semantic IDs) on-the-fly.\n    \n- **Methodology:** Use a frozen Large Multimodal Model (LMM) to generate \"Description Tokens\" for new items, and a lightweight adapter to map these tokens to the recommender's semantic codebook. This allows the System-2 planner to recommend items it has never seen during training.\n    \n\n#### RQ3: Can we implement \"Speculative Reasoning\" for cost control?\n\nNot every recommendation requires System-2 thinking. Recommending the next episode of a TV show is System-1. Recommending a vacation package is System-2.\n\n- **Hypothesis:** **\"Adaptive Cognitive Depth.\"** Use a lightweight gating network (System-1) to estimate the \"Complexity Risk\" of a request.\n    \n- **Methodology:**\n    \n    - If Risk < Threshold: Use DLRM (10ms).\n        \n    - If Risk > Threshold: Trigger R4ec/System-2 (500ms).\n        \n    - Use **Conformal Risk Control** to calibrate the gating threshold, ensuring that the probability of \"under-thinking\" a complex request is bounded by $\\alpha$.\n        \n\n### 6.3 Why This Problem?\n\nThis problem sits at the intersection of the three major trends identified in this report:\n\n1. **GenRec:** It addresses the \"Token Instability\" flaw of current GenRec models.\n    \n2. **System-2:** It operationalizes the theoretical gains of R4ec for real-world deployment.\n    \n3. **Trustworthiness:** It incorporates Conformal Risk Control not just for safety, but for computational efficiency (gating).\n    \n\nSolving this would effectively obsolete the current \"Distillation\" paradigm, allowing industrial systems to deploy genuine intelligence rather than compressed approximations of it.\n\n---\n\n## 7. Conclusion\n\nThe transition from 2024 to 2026 marks the end of the \"Accuracy Arms Race\" in Recommender Systems. We have reached the point of diminishing returns with purely discriminative models. The new frontier is **Cognitive and Generative**.\n\nThe collected evidence—from the unified architectures of **TIGER** and **MTGR**, to the reasoning frameworks of **R4ec** and **ThinkRec**, to the safety guarantees of **Conformal Risk Control**—points to a future where recommenders are not just ranking engines, but **agents** that understand, plan, and explain.\n\nHowever, this future is currently unevenly distributed. It exists in arXiv preprints and offline benchmarks like **AgentRecBench**, but it struggles to survive the harsh latency and cost constraints of the real world. The researcher who bridges this gap—by making reasoning efficient, dynamic, and safe—will define the standard for the next decade of personalized intelligence.\n\n### Table 2: Comparative Analysis of State-of-the-Art Paradigms (2025-2026)\n\n|**Feature**|**DLRM (Current Industry Standard)**|**Generative Retrieval (TIGER/LIGER)**|**System-2 Agents (R4ec/AgentCF)**|\n|---|---|---|---|\n|**Core Operation**|Discriminative Scoring ($u \\cdot i$)|Autoregressive Generation ($P(i_t \\\\| i_{<t}$)|Multi-step Planning & Reflection|\n|**Inference Latency**|**Ultra-Low (<10ms)**|High (Linear with token length)|Very High (Multiple LLM passes)|\n|**Cold Start**|Poor (Requires ID embeddings)|**Good** (Via Semantic IDs)|**Excellent** (Via Metadata Reasoning)|\n|**Vocabulary Handling**|**Robust** (Dynamic Embeddings)|**Fragile** (Token Instability)|Medium (Context Window Limits)|\n|**Explainability**|Low (Feature Importance)|Medium (Generated Text)|**High** (Chain-of-Thought)|\n|**Safety Guarantee**|Heuristic Filters|Implicit via Training Data|**Conformal Risk Control**|\n|**Primary Use Case**|High-throughput Feeds (TikTok/Ads)|Search & Long-tail Retrieval|Complex Decision Support (Travel/Finance)|\n\nThe path forward lies in blurring these columns: achieving the latency of DLRMs with the reasoning of Agents and the semantic understanding of Generative models. This is the **Latency-Efficient System-2 Reasoning** challenge.",
